module std.prelude

// The result of a three-way comparison (`a <=> b`). A sum type rather than the
// -1/0/1 integer Ruby and Perl return: an integer invites `if (a <=> b) == -1`,
// which is strictly worse than `a < b` and would leave the operator with no reason
// to exist. Named variants make the one thing `<=>` is *for* — handling all three
// outcomes in one place — the natural way to write it:
//
//   match guess <=> secret {
//     Less    => println("Too low!"),
//     Greater => println("Too high!"),
//     Equal   => println("You got it!"),
//   }
//
// and exhaustiveness then insists every case is covered, which is the property the
// `if`/`else if`/`else` chain it replaces cannot offer.
pub data Ordering = Less | Equal | Greater

// The trait that extends `<=>` beyond the primitives. A type that implements it can
// be compared three ways, and — once the relational operators are routed through it —
// ordered with `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=`.
//
// **One method, deliberately.** Everything else derives from `compare`, so an impl
// cannot make `<` and `<=>` disagree — the failure mode C++'s separate `operator<`
// and `operator<=>` and Java's `compareTo`-beside-`equals` both carry. The cost is
// that a type cannot supply a cheaper `<` than its `compare`; that is a trade worth
// making while correctness is the scarce thing.
//
// **Floats do not implement it, and that is the rule rather than an omission.** NaN
// is neither less than, equal to nor greater than anything, so a three-way answer has
// to pick one and every choice is wrong — the same reason `<=>` refuses floats
// outright. Ordering floats for a sort wants a `total_cmp` (bit-pattern order) that
// says in its name that it is not the arithmetic ordering.
//
// **Recognized by name and shape**, not by a marker: a `pub trait Ord` in the prelude
// whose single method matches `(Self, Self) -> Ordering`. That mirrors the fallback
// `canonical.go` already applies to *types* — an unmarked, canonically-shaped
// declaration of the right name — and is not the marker the design asked for, because
// an attribute does not parse on a trait declaration today. The marker is the better
// answer and is a grammar change; see todo.md.
pub trait Ord {
  compare: (Self, Self) -> Ordering
}

// Equality for the minority of types whose equality is **not** field-wise —
// case-insensitive text, a struct with a cache field that should not count, a set that
// compares by contents rather than by layout.
//
// **`==` does not require this trait.** Equality is structural by default and works on
// every type including a bare type variable, so implementing `Eq` *overrides* what
// `==` already does rather than enabling it. That is the opposite of Rust and Swift,
// and it follows from where this language started: unbounded structural equality
// already worked, and requiring a bound would have removed capability to gain
// ceremony.
//
// A primitive is never routed through an impl — `1 == 1` stays a machine comparison —
// so an `Eq` impl cannot change what equality means on the built-in types.
//
// **`Ord` deliberately does not require `Eq`.** The design first had `trait Ord: Eq`,
// on the reasoning that it stops `compare` answering `Equal` where equality says
// false. That is wrong here: equality is always available structurally, so demanding an
// *impl* of it would make `@derive(Ord)` — which synthesizes no `Eq` — fail on every
// type that used it. A type that implements both and lets them disagree is writing a
// bug the compiler cannot see; that is the residual cost of equality not being a
// bound.
pub trait Eq {
  eq: (Self, Self) -> bool
}
